- `.../tests/adapter.test.ts` — hand-rolled harness, 11 checks

## 2. How email is ingested

**Not IMAP, not webhook, not Gmail API — from this folder.** This folder is deliberately a *thin translation adapter* over an existing Gmail reader that lives in a **different repo** (`~/Desktop/dna-os-deadboard/supabase/functions/_shared/gmail-client.ts`, Deno/Supabase Edge, `gmail.readonly`). This connector makes exactly one authenticated `POST` and translates field names.

`adapter.ts:74` `fetchAlertEmails(config, query)`:
- `:75-77` fails `NOT_CONFIGURED` if endpoint/secret missing
- `:78-80` **query allowlist** — refuses anything not starting with `from:` / `subject:` / `newer_than:` / `{`, i.e. no open-inbox reads
- `:82-87` single `httpPostJson(endpoint_url, {[secret_header_name]: secret_value}, {query}, timeout)`
- `:89-95` maps transport/status to `UNREACHABLE` / `UNAUTHORIZED` / `UPSTREAM_ERROR`
- `:108-110` `parsed.messages.map(translate)`

Transport is `host-runtime.ts:64` `httpPostJson`, with a Node-16 fallback at `:36` `stdlibPostJson` (global `fetch` absent on this machine — documented as a live bug found 2026-08-26 where a live service was reported dead).

**Parsing** is minimal — there is no MIME parsing here at all, because the upstream already did `extractBodyText` + `getSubjectHeader`. The only real parsing is timestamp normalisation, `adapter.ts:34` `normaliseReceivedAt`, which prefers RFC3339 `date`, falls back to `internalDate` epoch-millis, and **throws rather than stamping `now`**:

```ts
throw new EmailSourceError("MALFORMED_RESPONSE", `message ${message.id} carries no parseable date or internalDate`);
```

## 3. Output shape, and where raw content is dropped

`adapter.ts:48` `translate` is the whole shaping step and the whole loss point:

```ts
return {
  message_id: message.id,
  thread_id: message.threadId,
  sender:     message.from ?? "",
  received_at: normaliseReceivedAt(message),
  subject:    message.subject ?? "",
  body:       message.bodyText ?? "",
};
```

Declared as the schema at `types.ts:87-103` (`FetchAlertEmailsResult`), envelope fields: `ok`, `emails`, `error_code`, `detail`, `source_endpoint`, `fetched_at`.

**What is dropped — and note it is dropped *upstream*, not here.** `UpstreamAlertMessage` (`types.ts:48-57`) is the entire vocabulary available to this folder, and it has only `id`, `threadId`, `internalDate`, `date`, `from`, `subject`, `bodyText`. There is **no** `to`/`cc`, no `Message-ID`/`References`/`In-Reply-To` header, no arbitrary header map, no HTML part, no MIME structure, no attachments, no `raw` RFC822/base64url payload, no Gmail `labelIds`/`snippet`/`historyId`. So exposing raw email content is not a change you can make in `translate` alone — the upstream contract must carry it first, which ties into the `NOT_YET_BUILT` note below.

Also lossy: `from` and `subject` and `bodyText` all collapse `undefined` to `""` (`adapter.ts:55-58`), so "absent" and "empty" are indistinguishable downstream.

## 4. Notion of "provider"

**There is no provider concept in code.** No `providers.ts`, no provider field on any type, no routing, no per-vendor parser. Provider selection is entirely implicit in the caller-supplied `query` string, e.g. `"from:notify.railway.app"` (`tests/adapter.test.ts:63, 67`). The output records carry no `provider` field — a downstream consumer must re-derive it from `sender`.

Provider *does* exist as prior art elsewhere: `README.md:28-32` notes that `subscription-email-sync` (in the other repo) "already keeps a provider table of deterministic `from:` fragments (Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, RunPod, Vercel, GitHub, Apple, Hostinger)" — and explicitly that **Railway is not in that table** (`README.md:66`).

## 5. Public API surface

`contract.ts` is the sole boundary — one function, one error class, five types:

```ts
export { fetchAlertEmails } from "./adapter";
export { EmailSourceError } from "./types";
export type { EmailSourceConfig, EmailSourceErrorCode, FetchAlertEmailsResult, UpstreamAlertMessage, UpstreamResponse } from "./types";
```

`contract.ts:7-8` states an explicit rule: **never re-export `host-runtime.ts`** — `httpPostJson` stays private so no caller can aim the adapter at an arbitrary host.

Config schema `types.ts:28-40`: `endpoint_url`, `secret_header_name`, `secret_value` (caller-supplied, never read from a credential store here), optional `timeout_ms` (default 20s, `adapter.ts:86`). Error codes `types.ts:65-70`: `NOT_CONFIGURED | UNAUTHORIZED | UPSTREAM_ERROR | UNREACHABLE | MALFORMED_RESPONSE`. `fetchAlertEmails` **always returns, never throws**.

Downstream consumer is `control/alert-correlation/contract.ts` → `runAlertCorrelation(emails)` (imported at `tests/adapter.test.ts:20`); I could not read it due to sandbox EPERM, but the test shows it accepts the six-field record array directly and returns reports with `.service`, `.assessment.incident_state`, `.assessment.observation`, `.current_real_state`.

