This is deliberately **not** an email reader. It is a thin *input adapter* that makes one authenticated HTTP call to an already-deployed Gmail reader and renames fields. From `README.md:3-15`:

> **หน้าที่:** the EMAIL INPUT ADAPTER between the **existing** Gmail reader and `control/alert-correlation/`. It reads no mail. It performs one authenticated HTTP call and translates field names.

Pipeline: existing Deno/Supabase Edge function (`dna-os-deadboard/supabase/functions/_shared/gmail-client.ts`) → HTTP + shared secret → this adapter (Node) → `RawAlertEmail[]` → `control/alert-correlation/`.

The dominant design constraint is a credential boundary (`types.ts:10-15`): no OAuth flow, no IMAP/SMTP, no `googleapis` dependency, no Google credential in this repo. The secret is supplied *by the caller*, not read from a credential store here.

Important status note in `README.md:60-81`: **the upstream endpoint does not exist yet** ("NOT_YET_BUILT"). No deployed function returns raw provider-alert messages. Until someone writes a sibling Edge Function returning `{ ok, messages: [{ id, threadId, internalDate, from, subject, bodyText }] }`, `fetchAlertEmails` always returns `NOT_CONFIGURED`/`UNREACHABLE`.

## 2. Ingestion, parsing, normalization

All in `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/adapter.ts`:

- **`fetchAlertEmails(config, query)` — line 74** (the only exported function). Flow: validate config → validate query scope → `httpPostJson` → status triage → `JSON.parse` → `.map(translate)`.
- **`translate(message)` — line 48.** The entire field mapping, upstream Gmail shape → downstream shape:
  ```
  id -> message_id, threadId -> thread_id, from -> sender,
  subject -> subject, bodyText -> body, (date|internalDate) -> received_at
  ```
  Note the lossy defaults: `sender: message.from ?? ""`, `subject: ... ?? ""`, `body: message.bodyText ?? ""`. Missing `id`/`threadId` throws `MALFORMED_RESPONSE`.
- **`normaliseReceivedAt(message)` — line 34.** Prefers RFC3339 `date`, falls back to `internalDate` epoch-millis. Deliberately throws rather than defaulting to now (lines 42-45):
  > No provable timestamp. Refuse rather than stamping "now", which would silently make an old alert look like it just arrived and would break alert-correlation's claim-vs-observation time comparison.
- **`failure(config, code, detail)` — line 62.** Builds the always-empty-`emails` failure record with provenance.

There is **no provider-specific parsing** anywhere — no provider table, no regex per-vendor extraction, no body parsing. "Provider" scoping is pushed entirely into the caller-supplied Gmail `query` string, guarded at `adapter.ts:78`:
```ts
if (!query || !/^(from:|subject:|newer_than:|\{)/i.test(query.trim())) {
```
An unscoped query (`""`, `is:unread`) is rejected as `NOT_CONFIGURED`. The README (lines 28-32) notes the existing `subscription-email-sync` holds the provider `from:` fragment table (Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, RunPod, Vercel, GitHub, Apple, Hostinger) — and that **Railway is not in it**.

Transport, `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/host-runtime.ts`:
- `nowIso()` — line 17; `httpPostJson(url, headers, body, timeoutMs)` — line 64; `stdlibPostJson` — line 36.
- Non-obvious detail worth carrying forward (lines 29-35): global `fetch` is absent on this machine (Node 16), which was silently reporting a live host as dead. `httpPostJson:73` branches to a `node:http`/`node:https` implementation when `typeof fetch !== "function"`. Note the `AbortController`/`setTimeout` at lines 70-71 only governs the `fetch` path; the stdlib path uses its own `timeout` option.

