## 2. How alerts are ingested — and what "provider alert emails" means

**Nothing in this folder reads mail. That is the entire design premise.** No IMAP, no SMTP, no Gmail API, no OAuth, no webhook receiver. The pipeline (`README.md:7-15`):

```
EXISTING GMAIL READER  (dna-os-deadboard/supabase/functions/_shared/gmail-client.ts, Deno)
        ↓  HTTP + shared secret
EMAIL INPUT ADAPTER    (this folder, Node)
        ↓  RawAlertEmail[]
control/alert-correlation/
```

Ingestion is **one authenticated HTTP POST** to a Supabase Edge Function that already owns the Gmail `gmail.readonly` credential. `adapter.ts:82-87`:

```ts
const response = await httpPostJson(
  config.endpoint_url,
  { [config.secret_header_name]: config.secret_value },
  { query },
  config.timeout_ms ?? 20_000,
);
```

The request body is literally just `{ query }` — a Gmail search fragment. "Provider alert emails" means **sender-scoped** messages from infra providers (Railway, Vercel, GitHub, OpenAI, Anthropic, RunPod, Google, Apple, Hostinger per `README.md:28-31`). The test's live example is a Railway build-failure alert from `hello@notify.railway.app` (`tests/adapter.test.ts:75-82`).

**Query scoping is enforced, not advisory** (`adapter.ts:78-80`):

```ts
if (!query || !/^(from:|subject:|newer_than:|\{)/i.test(query.trim())) {
  return failure(config, "NOT_CONFIGURED", `refusing an unscoped query ${JSON.stringify(query)} — a query must start with a deterministic filter (from:/subject:/newer_than:), never an open inbox read`);
}
```

### Parsing / transformation

There is no MIME parsing here — the upstream already flattened the message. The whole transform is `translate()` at `adapter.ts:48-60`:

```ts
function translate(message: UpstreamAlertMessage): FetchAlertEmailsResult["emails"][number] {
  if (!message.id || !message.threadId) {
    throw new EmailSourceError("MALFORMED_RESPONSE", "upstream message is missing id or threadId");
  }
  return {
    message_id: message.id,
    thread_id: message.threadId,
    sender: message.from ?? "",
    received_at: normaliseReceivedAt(message),
    subject: message.subject ?? "",
    body: message.bodyText ?? "",
  };
}
```

Plus timestamp normalisation at `adapter.ts:34-46`, which notably **refuses to invent a timestamp**:

```ts
function normaliseReceivedAt(message: UpstreamAlertMessage): string {
  if (message.date && !Number.isNaN(Date.parse(message.date))) return new Date(message.date).toISOString();
  if (message.internalDate) {
    const millis = Number(message.internalDate);
    if (Number.isFinite(millis) && millis > 0) return new Date(millis).toISOString();
  }
  throw new EmailSourceError("MALFORMED_RESPONSE", `message ${message.id} carries no parseable date or internalDate`);
}
```

