## 1. What this connector is

It is not a reader. It is a **pure field-translation adapter** sitting between a Gmail reader that lives in a *different* repo/runtime and a downstream correlator.

`/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/README.md:3-15` states the chain:

```
EXISTING GMAIL READER  (dna-os-deadboard/supabase/functions/_shared/gmail-client.ts, Deno)
        ↓  HTTP + shared secret
EMAIL INPUT ADAPTER    (this folder, Node)
        ↓  RawAlertEmail[]
control/alert-correlation/
```

Complete file inventory (6 files; confirmed by probing — the test's own source list at `tests/adapter.test.ts:39` names exactly the four `.ts` sources):

- `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/README.md`
- `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/contract.ts` — public boundary, re-exports only
- `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/adapter.ts` — the logic
- `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/types.ts` — all type contracts
- `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/host-runtime.ts` — one HTTP POST + a clock
- `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/tests/adapter.test.ts`

There is **no** `package.json`, `manifest.yaml`, `schema.json`, `pyproject.toml`, or config file in this folder. `host-runtime.ts:10` refers to "Node >=18 per package.json engines", but that `package.json` is at a repo level I cannot read. Config is not a file — it is the `EmailSourceConfig` argument passed in by the caller (`types.ts:28-40`), deliberately so the credential never lands in this repo (`types.ts:33-38`).

Entrypoint is `fetchAlertEmails` exported at `contract.ts:11`. Note `contract.ts:7-8`: `host-runtime.ts` must never be re-exported, so `httpPostJson` stays private.

## 2. How it fetches and processes

It does **one** authenticated POST and nothing else — `adapter.ts:82-87`:

```ts
const response = await httpPostJson(
  config.endpoint_url,
  { [config.secret_header_name]: config.secret_value },
  { query },
  config.timeout_ms ?? 20_000,
);
```

The request body is literally just `{ query }`. There is no field-selection / format / projection parameter in the wire protocol at all — which matters directly for your raw-content question (see §3).

The "provider alert" concept is **only a query convention, not code**. There is no provider table, no per-provider parser, no regex extraction in this folder. Scoping is enforced by a single guard, `adapter.ts:78-80`:

```ts
if (!query || !/^(from:|subject:|newer_than:|\{)/i.test(query.trim())) {
  return failure(config, "NOT_CONFIGURED", `refusing an unscoped query ...`);
}
```

`README.md:28-32` says the provider table (Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, RunPod, Vercel, GitHub, Apple, Hostinger) lives upstream in `subscription-email-sync`, and `README.md:66` notes Railway is *not* in it.

Normalization is confined to timestamps, `adapter.ts:33-46`. It prefers RFC3339 `date`, falls back to `internalDate` epoch-millis, and **throws rather than defaulting to now**:

```ts
throw new EmailSourceError("MALFORMED_RESPONSE", `message ${message.id} carries no parseable date or internalDate`);
```

HTTP transport is in `host-runtime.ts:64-91`, with a Node-16 fallback path (`stdlibPostJson`, `host-runtime.ts:36-62`) because global `fetch` was found missing on the live machine (`host-runtime.ts:29-35`). Errors are always returned, never thrown, per `types.ts:81-86`; error codes are the five in `types.ts:65-70`.

## 3. Emitted data shape — raw content is dropped, and dropped twice

There are no "streams" in the Airbyte/singer sense. One function returns one result object, `types.ts:87-103`. The emitted record is a **closed, anonymous, 6-field inline type** with no index signature and no extension point:

```ts
readonly emails: readonly {
  readonly message_id: string;
  readonly thread_id: string;
  readonly sender: string;
  readonly received_at: string;
  readonly subject: string;
  readonly body: string;
}[];
```

The mapping is `translate()` at `adapter.ts:48-60`:

```ts
return {
  message_id: message.id,
  thread_id: message.threadId,
  sender: message.from ?? "",
  received_at: normaliseReceivedAt(message),
  subject: message.subject ?? "",
  body: message.bodyText ?? "",
};
```

**Raw message content is lost at two separate layers, and this is the key constraint for your task:**

1. **At the upstream contract.** `UpstreamAlertMessage` (`types.ts:48-57`) has only `id`, `threadId`, `internalDate?`, `date?`, `from?`, `subject?`, `bodyText?`. There are no headers, no `payload`, no MIME parts, no `raw`. `types.ts:44-46` explains why: the shape mirrors what the Deno reader already produces via `fetchGmailMessage` + `getSubjectHeader` + `extractBodyText` — i.e. the body has **already been flattened to a single text string upstream**, before this adapter ever sees it. `README.md:70` pins the same wire shape: `{ ok, messages: [{ id, threadId, internalDate, from, subject, bodyText }] }`.

2. **At translation.** `translate()` is a whitelist. Even the fields the upstream *does* send are partly discarded — `date` and the original `internalDate` string are consumed by `normaliseReceivedAt` and never emitted; only the derived `received_at` survives. Any extra field an upstream added would be silently dropped, because the return type is closed.

So exposing headers / MIME parts is not a local edit: it requires (a) widening `UpstreamAlertMessage`, (b) widening the emitted record in `types.ts:90-97`, and (c) a change to the upstream endpoint that **does not exist yet** — `README.md:60-77` marks it `NOT_YET_BUILT`, and explicitly says writing it was withheld pending Owner go-ahead because it means deploying into a production repo handling bank/payment data. Until then `fetchAlertEmails` returns `NOT_CONFIGURED` (`adapter.ts:75-77`).

## 4. Existing "raw" / passthrough notion

Within this folder: **none.** The only occurrences of the word are `RawAlertEmail` (`README.md:12`, `README.md:81`) — which is the *downstream* `control/alert-correlation/` input type name, and it denotes the already-normalized 6-field record, not a raw MIME payload. There is no `raw`, no passthrough mode, no `include_headers`-style flag, no config switch anywhere in `types.ts`, `adapter.ts`, or `contract.ts`. The request body `{ query }` (`adapter.ts:85`) carries no mode parameter.

I could **not** verify whether a raw/passthrough concept exists elsewhere in the repo — every path outside this folder returns EPERM under the current sandbox. Worth re-running that search with filesystem access before designing; the likely places are `control/alert-correlation/types.ts` (the real `RawAlertEmail` definition) and any sibling connector under `connections/`.

## 5. Tests and testing patterns

Single file, hand-rolled harness, no framework: `/Users/tam/Desktop/DNA-OS-INFRASTRUCTURE/connections/email-alert-source/tests/adapter.test.ts`. A `check(name, condition, detail)` counter (lines 22-27) and a pass/fail tally on exit (lines 106-111). Run via `npx tsx connections/email-alert-source/tests/adapter.test.ts` (`README.md:87`, expecting 11 passed). Tests are grouped A-E by comment banner.

Three patterns matter if you add raw-content fields:

**(a) The source-scan tests will gate your change.** `adapter.test.ts:39-53` reads the four source files off disk and regex-scans them:

```ts
const sources = ["adapter.ts", "types.ts", "host-runtime.ts", "contract.ts"];
const readerMarkers = /oauth2\.googleapis|gmail\.googleapis|googleapis\.com|users\.messages|refresh_token|GMAIL_CLIENT|nodemailer|imapflow|require\(["']imap|from\s+["']imap/i;
```

A1 strips comments first (`adapter.test.ts:47`) — the rationale at lines 40-46 is that these files document what they *don't* do, so raw-text scanning would false-positive. A2 (line 53) scans **raw, uncommented** text for `GMAIL_(CLIENT_ID|CLIENT_SECRET|REFRESH_TOKEN)`. Practical consequence: a field named `raw`, `headers`, or `mime_parts` is fine, but do not write `users.messages` or `gmail.googleapis` even in a comment inside `adapter.ts`/`types.ts` — A1 strips comments but A2 does not, and A1's marker list would still catch such a token in live code.

Note also that `sources` is a hardcoded list of four filenames. **A new file (e.g. `raw-parts.ts`) would not be scanned at all** — a coverage gap if the raw-passthrough logic lands in a new module.

**(b) The translation mapping is not actually exercised.** `translate()` is module-private (not exported from `adapter.ts`, not re-exported in `contract.ts`). Test E does not call it — it *re-implements* the mapping by hand at `adapter.test.ts:85-92`:

```ts
const translated = [{
  message_id: upstreamShaped.id,
  thread_id: upstreamShaped.threadId,
  ...
}];
```

So E1-E4 assert on a hand-built literal, not on adapter output. If you add raw fields to `translate()`, this test will keep passing while covering nothing new; you would need either to export `translate` or to stub the HTTP layer.

